      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "There is no single definitive answer, as most political philosophers like Rousseau and Hobbes argue that reasonable limits on the social contract must exist to preserve the very freedom the contract was meant to protect. Historically, governments have often justified expanded surveillance as a necessary evolution of their duties to ensure order, yet critics consistently view such actions as violations when they trample individual liberty for the sake of collective security. Your notes should reflect this enduring tension rather than presenting one side as absolute truth, as the validity ultimately depends on which principles—individual autonomy or social stability—you prioritize.",
